# Week 1: Project Scope
**Project:** ScholarshipScan: Automatic Extraction and Comparison of Eligibility Criteria from Malaysian Scholarship Documents
**Course:** AI Engineering, Topic 1 Lab
 
## 1. Purpose
ScholarshipScan reads public scholarship documents and turns their eligibility criteria, deadlines, values and required documents into a structured, source-linked comparison table for students.
 
## 2. Target Users
Post-SPM, STPM and matriculation students in Malaysia, and their parents or school counsellors, who need to compare scholarships without reading many long documents.
 
## 3. Why AI Is Needed
- Criteria are written in free text, in English and Malay, with a different structure for each sponsor.
- Criteria are often conditional ("and", "or", "except"), and one paragraph can hold several rules.
- Keyword search or fixed rules will miss many phrasings. An LLM with a fixed output schema can handle the variation, and the result can be measured against a baseline.
## 4. In Scope
- Collect 20 to 30 public scholarship documents (PDF or web pages) from at least 2 to 3 sources.
- Extract these fields into JSON: scholarship name, sponsor, eligibility (citizenship, age, minimum results, field of study), deadline, value or benefits, required documents, source URL, date checked.
- Support English and Malay text.
- Show a comparison table with a link to the source for every field.
- Evaluate against a manually labelled ground truth and a regex baseline.
## 5. Out of Scope
- Deciding whether a specific student is eligible or will be accepted.
- Personal profile matching or ranking of scholarships for a user.
- Submitting applications or tracking deadlines for users.
- A general-purpose chatbot.
- Scholarships that require a login, or documents whose terms of use forbid collection.
## 6. Inputs and Outputs
**Input:** a scholarship document (PDF or HTML) converted to text.
**Output:** a JSON record per scholarship, shown as a comparison table.
 
Example:
 
| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Citizenship | Malaysian |
| Age limit | (extracted value) |
| Minimum results | (extracted value) |
| Deadline | (extracted value) |
| Source | URL + date checked |
 
## 7. Data Sources
1. MEXT Scholarship 2027 (KOSEN), Embassy of Japan in Malaysia: https://www.my.emb-japan.go.jp/files/100990158.pdf
2. MEXT Scholarship 2027 (Undergraduate), Embassy of Japan in Malaysia: https://www.my.emb-japan.go.jp/files/100990174.pdf
3. Malaysian sponsor pages (e.g. JPA or MARA): URL to be added after checking terms of use.
Each document is recorded with its URL, download date and intake year. Where redistribution is unclear, only URLs and download scripts are stored in the repository.
 
## 8. Evaluation Plan (preview, details in Topic 2)
- Ground truth: 30 to 50 scholarships labelled manually (6 to 8 fields each).
- Metrics: precision, recall and F1 per field; faithfulness rate (extracted value appears in the source text).
- Baseline: regex and keyword rules; compare against the LLM approach.
- Error analysis: identify which fields and phrasings fail most often.
## 9. Success Criteria
- The LLM approach scores higher than the regex baseline on overall F1.
- Faithfulness rate is reported, and unsupported values are flagged.
- Every table cell links back to its source document.
## 10. Constraints and Assumptions
- One semester (14 weeks) and a single developer.
- Only publicly available documents are used.
- Scholarship details change every year, so each record stores its intake year and date checked.
## 11. Responsible AI Risks (summary)
| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Wrong extraction could make a student miss an opportunity | Source link and date on every field, faithfulness check, "verify with sponsor" notice |
| Outdated information | Store intake year and download date, flag old documents |
| Coverage bias (only large or English-language sponsors) | Include Malay documents and report sponsor coverage |
| Copyright and terms of use | Check each site's terms, store URLs and scripts where unclear |
 
